WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:04.080
Imagine a calculator that remembers every single mistake you've ever made.

00:04.180 --> 00:05.120
Oh, that sounds stressful.

00:05.340 --> 00:05.480
Right.

00:05.560 --> 00:08.080
Like every typo, every time you cleared the screen,

00:08.600 --> 00:11.580
every frantic backspace you hit when you realized you carried the wrong number,

00:11.800 --> 00:14.840
all of it just permanently etched into its hardware.

00:15.860 --> 00:17.760
You'd probably call it a nightmare, honestly.

00:17.980 --> 00:19.140
Most people would, yeah.

00:19.540 --> 00:22.960
But there's this independent researcher named Flaconian

00:22.960 --> 00:26.100
who calls it the absolute future of computer science.

00:26.100 --> 00:30.320
It really is a complete inversion of how we think about digital space.

00:30.520 --> 00:36.300
I mean, we are so accustomed to the idea that computers are fundamentally designed to forget things.

00:36.660 --> 00:36.760
Right.

00:37.420 --> 00:39.620
Usually when we think about computers doing their thing,

00:39.860 --> 00:43.340
you know, running a program, rendering a video game, doing basic math,

00:43.880 --> 00:46.500
there is this baked-in expectation of finality.

00:46.720 --> 00:47.120
Exactly.

00:47.520 --> 00:48.080
It's ephemeral.

00:48.160 --> 00:48.720
It's ephemeral.

00:49.180 --> 00:49.760
Yeah, ephemeral.

00:50.380 --> 00:53.080
You type 2 plus 2, you hit equals, the screen says 4,

00:53.080 --> 00:56.520
and those initial inputs just kind of vanish into the ether.

00:56.900 --> 00:57.160
They're gone.

00:57.900 --> 00:58.800
It's efficient, right.

00:59.880 --> 01:03.560
I mean, we built our entire digital world on this premise of overriding memory

01:03.560 --> 01:06.420
just to save physical space on the silicon chips.

01:06.880 --> 01:09.340
We did, but, you know, the cost of that efficiency

01:09.340 --> 01:12.420
is that the system has absolutely no memory of its own past.

01:12.560 --> 01:13.040
Not at all.

01:13.300 --> 01:15.460
You only ever see the present state of the machine.

01:15.460 --> 01:18.520
And when you step into the world of, like, software debugging

01:18.520 --> 01:23.700
or forensic software analysis, suddenly that clean calculator feels like a crime scene.

01:24.100 --> 01:25.300
Oh, that's a great way to put it.

01:25.400 --> 01:29.400
Yeah, a crime scene where the evidence has been deliberately wiped clean.

01:29.960 --> 01:34.660
We are looking at a computational landscape that is honestly amnesiac.

01:34.880 --> 01:35.560
Very much so.

01:35.920 --> 01:38.140
Which brings us to the foundation of this deep dive.

01:39.060 --> 01:42.220
Today we are exploring a dense, really fascinating,

01:42.400 --> 01:44.960
2026 graduate text by Flyxion.

01:44.960 --> 01:49.660
It's titled Constructing Programming Languages from SpherePop Primitives.

01:50.300 --> 01:52.220
It is a truly paradigm-shifting piece of work.

01:52.340 --> 01:57.200
I mean, Flyxion is proposing a completely different foundational physics for computation.

01:57.620 --> 01:58.260
It's wild.

01:58.900 --> 02:03.600
And our mission today is to kind of bypass all the dense academic jargon in this paper

02:03.600 --> 02:07.740
and uncover a revolutionary way to understand how programming languages

02:07.740 --> 02:09.800
actually work under the hood.

02:09.960 --> 02:10.200
Right.

02:10.340 --> 02:12.400
Getting past the math and into the mechanics.

02:12.760 --> 02:13.120
Exactly.

02:13.120 --> 02:18.880
We are going to explore how treating computer code as an indestructible, immutable ledger

02:18.880 --> 02:22.000
completely changes our understanding of everything.

02:22.640 --> 02:23.880
And we really mean everything.

02:24.180 --> 02:24.360
Yeah.

02:24.560 --> 02:28.660
From the virtual computers of the 1980s all the way to modern complex package managers.

02:29.200 --> 02:30.180
It's a huge spectrum.

02:30.460 --> 02:36.040
So if you're listening right now, maybe you are prepping for a massive systems architecture meeting,

02:36.160 --> 02:41.460
or maybe you're just insanely curious about how the digital world operates when you peel back the layers.

02:41.460 --> 02:45.480
We want to give you a shortcut to being incredibly well-informed on this.

02:45.720 --> 02:45.840
Right.

02:45.920 --> 02:47.300
Without drowning in info overload.

02:48.220 --> 02:54.480
Because honestly, reading this paper felt less like an engineering textbook and more like a thrilling detective story.

02:54.940 --> 02:58.360
A detective story about forensic accounting for code.

02:58.680 --> 02:59.080
Exactly.

02:59.080 --> 03:05.160
That's a really great framing because it is entirely about preserving the physical evidence of computation.

03:05.420 --> 03:13.540
So before we can look at the programming languages themselves, we need to understand the weird, brilliant universe they are being built inside.

03:14.000 --> 03:14.460
The kernel.

03:14.460 --> 03:16.880
Yeah, Flyxion calls it the SpherePop kernel.

03:17.300 --> 03:20.940
And the paper claims this entire universe is built on just four verbs.

03:21.200 --> 03:21.700
Just four.

03:22.320 --> 03:23.540
I'm looking at this list.

03:23.920 --> 03:25.620
Bind, pop, refuse, collapse.

03:26.320 --> 03:30.220
And I'm struggling to see how you build a whole operating system out of just four actions.

03:30.420 --> 03:32.100
It sounds impossible at first glance.

03:32.420 --> 03:33.780
Like, bind makes sense to me.

03:33.860 --> 03:34.460
It's birth.

03:34.880 --> 03:36.800
But how do the others do the heavy lifting?

03:36.960 --> 03:38.740
Well, let's walk through the mechanics of that universe.

03:39.220 --> 03:42.100
In our normal computers, memory is basically a chalkboard.

03:42.100 --> 03:45.240
You write a number, you erase it, you write a new one.

03:45.460 --> 03:50.380
But in the SpherePop universe, there are absolutely no erasers.

03:51.300 --> 03:59.560
The core concept is a world that contains an append-only history, so the ledger, and a family of live spheres.

03:59.900 --> 04:02.620
And a sphere being like a piece of data?

04:02.780 --> 04:03.960
A piece of data, yeah.

04:04.080 --> 04:08.560
But more specifically, it is an immutable record of a birth event.

04:09.040 --> 04:09.320
Okay.

04:09.580 --> 04:11.240
Immutable meaning it cannot be changed.

04:11.240 --> 04:11.480
Right.

04:12.100 --> 04:16.020
When a piece of data is created, it is born as a live sphere.

04:16.560 --> 04:25.060
It has an ID, a tag telling us what kind of data it is, a key for looking it up, a payload containing the actual data.

04:25.280 --> 04:26.140
The meat of it, yeah.

04:26.240 --> 04:26.600
Exactly.

04:27.120 --> 04:29.480
And crucially, a tuple of dependencies.

04:30.200 --> 04:34.060
It is permanently coupled to the older spheres it was created from.

04:34.380 --> 04:35.820
So it knows where it came from.

04:35.920 --> 04:36.160
Always.

04:36.160 --> 04:41.000
Now, regarding those four primitives, you're right that bind is birth.

04:41.000 --> 04:46.260
It introduces a new live sphere into the world, appending that birth event to the ledger.

04:46.620 --> 04:47.540
And it consumes nothing.

04:47.960 --> 04:48.200
Right.

04:48.720 --> 04:50.460
The second primitive is pop.

04:50.960 --> 04:53.760
Like, how does pop work if we can't delete anything?

04:54.160 --> 04:55.500
Because popping usually means removing.

04:55.500 --> 04:59.520
It does remove a live sphere from the active, queryable family.

04:59.520 --> 05:02.020
But it doesn't delete it from existence.

05:02.280 --> 05:02.820
Oh, I see.

05:02.940 --> 05:04.180
It commits it to history.

05:04.440 --> 05:10.280
It's the system's way of saying, you know, this piece of data has successfully completed its purpose and is no longer active.

05:10.760 --> 05:12.880
It appends a pop event to the ledger.

05:12.880 --> 05:13.940
Okay, but wait.

05:14.320 --> 05:15.760
What if the code encounters an error?

05:15.960 --> 05:16.160
Ah.

05:16.480 --> 05:19.340
Because you can't just pop a success if the operation failed.

05:20.000 --> 05:27.600
Like, if a program tries to divide by zero or looks for a file that isn't there, you need a different way to handle that data's lifecycle, right?

05:28.200 --> 05:31.800
You've hit on the exact reason for the third primitive, which is refuse.

05:32.240 --> 05:32.520
Okay.

05:32.720 --> 05:33.160
Refuse.

05:34.000 --> 05:37.940
This action also removes a live sphere from the active family, just like pop.

05:38.300 --> 05:40.940
However, it does it for a negative reason.

05:41.160 --> 05:42.000
So it's an exclusion.

05:42.480 --> 05:42.960
Exactly.

05:43.240 --> 05:49.200
It excludes a sphere and it actually must record a specific mandatory reason for its exclusion.

05:49.440 --> 05:49.740
Oh, wow.

05:50.200 --> 05:50.400
Yeah.

05:50.540 --> 05:57.060
The sphere and its payload stay in the history forever, marked as refused, with the documented explanation of exactly what went wrong.

05:57.660 --> 06:01.100
It's like an error log that you can never, ever delete or tamper with.

06:01.220 --> 06:01.480
Yes.

06:01.480 --> 06:04.880
It just forces the system to admit its failures on the permanent record.

06:05.120 --> 06:05.640
It really does.

06:05.860 --> 06:07.920
And then the fourth primitive is collapse.

06:08.120 --> 06:08.620
Collapse, right.

06:08.620 --> 06:16.880
Collapse takes a read-only view of the world at a specific moment in time, evaluates a rule based on that view, and publishes a digest of the result.

06:17.040 --> 06:17.900
So it doesn't change anything.

06:18.000 --> 06:20.220
It changes absolutely nothing about the spheres themselves.

06:20.360 --> 06:23.560
It just records that an observation was made by an outside entity.

06:23.560 --> 06:35.280
Okay, so just to recap, bind creates the data, pop retires it successfully, refuse retires it with an error code, and collapse just looks at it.

06:35.440 --> 06:37.120
That's the entire reference kernel.

06:37.120 --> 06:39.540
That is the whole operating system.

06:40.200 --> 06:40.820
That's wild.

06:41.020 --> 06:41.380
It is.

06:42.200 --> 06:49.040
And the reason this matters so deeply is that state, like the current memory of the computer, is never stored beside the ledger.

06:49.500 --> 06:50.360
Wait, so where is it?

06:50.780 --> 06:55.480
The state is reconstructed purely by reading the history of these four primitives.

06:55.760 --> 06:56.340
Oh, I get it.

06:56.620 --> 07:01.520
Traditional computers overwrite memory constantly, destroying the evidence of what just happened to save RAM.

07:01.940 --> 07:02.760
Yes, exactly.

07:02.760 --> 07:07.720
But the SpherePop model forces languages to keep perfect, indisputable receipts.

07:08.040 --> 07:09.060
Perfect receipts.

07:09.500 --> 07:16.720
It really makes me think of a meticulous, obsessive accountant who flat out refuses to use erasers or whiteout.

07:17.300 --> 07:18.360
That's a good analogy.

07:18.740 --> 07:23.120
Like if a mistake is made or a number needs to be updated, they don't delete the old number.

07:23.220 --> 07:31.380
They just draw a single red line through it, write the new number next to it, and write a detailed footnote at the bottom of the page explaining exactly why the change was made.

07:31.760 --> 07:32.100
Yes.

07:32.100 --> 07:35.060
And who made it, and what source documents they used.

07:35.160 --> 07:38.440
It just leaves a permanent paper trail of the error and the correction.

07:39.040 --> 07:42.940
I really like that analogy because it highlights the auditability of the system.

07:43.560 --> 07:53.200
Because every event is appended in a chain as it happens, a consumer can verify the final result of a computation without having to blindly trust the processor that produced it.

07:53.860 --> 07:55.620
Because you can just check the math yourself.

07:55.900 --> 07:56.360
Exactly.

07:56.640 --> 08:00.280
You can replay the entire ledger, step by step, and prove it matches.

08:00.280 --> 08:08.940
So now that we have this indestructible ledger, this obsessive accountant, how do we prove it actually represents a physical computer?

08:09.620 --> 08:11.120
Well, you start at the very bottom.

08:11.620 --> 08:11.840
Right.

08:12.020 --> 08:15.880
Because before Flyxion even gets to software, the text starts with hardware.

08:15.880 --> 08:18.760
Specifically, a 4-bit shift register.

08:18.760 --> 08:20.620
Yes, the shift register.

08:20.820 --> 08:27.720
Now, for those of us who aren't electrical engineers, what is the physical reality of a shift register, and why start there?

08:28.040 --> 08:33.140
A shift register is one of the most fundamental building blocks in digital electronics.

08:33.880 --> 08:38.900
Physically, imagine a small silicon chip with a row of tiny storage cells.

08:39.100 --> 08:39.260
Okay.

08:39.260 --> 08:47.300
Each capable of holding a single bit, a 1 or a 0. These are connected in a line. In a 4-bit register, you have 4 cells.

08:47.620 --> 08:48.420
Right. 4 slots.

08:48.660 --> 08:54.060
Let's say they currently hold the sequence 1, 0, 1, 0. There is also a hardware clock.

08:54.260 --> 08:56.000
Like a literal ticking clock.

08:56.220 --> 08:59.620
Well, an oscillator sending a pulsing electrical signal.

09:00.160 --> 09:01.540
Oh, the heartbeat of the computer.

09:01.540 --> 09:14.040
A heartbeat. Exactly. Every time that electrical pulse hits its peak, which we call the clock edge, the register takes a new bit in from a wire on the left side, shifts all the existing bits over one spot to the right.

09:14.220 --> 09:15.200
And the one on the end.

09:15.620 --> 09:21.300
The bit on the far right is physically pushed out. The voltage holding it simply drops. The data is just gone.

09:21.900 --> 09:29.520
Okay. So if the register is holding 1, 0, 1, 0, and we push a 0 in from the left on the clock tick, the whole thing slides right.

09:29.780 --> 09:30.060
Yes.

09:30.060 --> 09:33.160
So the new state becomes 0, 1, 0, 1.

09:33.440 --> 09:41.740
Now consider the physical reality of that exact moment. In traditional computer science, we abstract this away. We just care about the new state.

09:42.060 --> 09:42.280
Right.

09:42.520 --> 09:51.120
But in the SpherePop reconstruction, Flyxion models the actual physics of that clock edge. The system binds the new input bit as a sphere.

09:51.880 --> 09:52.400
Okay.

09:53.120 --> 09:59.260
Then, for each of the four slots in the register, it binds a new sphere, looking at the old bit to its left.

09:59.260 --> 10:04.380
Then, it pops all the old spheres because they've been consumed by the clock tick.

10:04.480 --> 10:04.960
Because they're done.

10:05.200 --> 10:12.140
Right. Finally, it uses collapse to publish the new four-bit word so the outside world can actually observe it.

10:12.600 --> 10:18.660
But wait, here is where the forensic accounting comes in. Let's say my register is holding 1, 0, 1, 0.

10:18.660 --> 10:22.840
I shift in a 0. The new state is 0, 1, 0, 1.

10:23.080 --> 10:23.360
Right.

10:23.760 --> 10:28.920
Now, what if my register was originally holding 1, 0, 1, 1, and I shift in a 0?

10:29.720 --> 10:33.400
The new state is, wait, also 0, 1, 0, 1.

10:33.600 --> 10:35.820
The exact same observable state.

10:36.080 --> 10:36.340
Wow.

10:36.340 --> 10:46.460
In a traditional simulation, if you only look at the current state, 0, 1, 0, 1, you have absolutely no idea whether the previous state ended in a 0 or a 1.

10:46.820 --> 10:50.940
Because the information that fell off the right edge, the lost bit, is permanently destroyed.

10:51.440 --> 10:51.960
Exactly.

10:52.440 --> 10:56.240
But not in SpherePop. How does it handle the lost bit?

10:56.780 --> 11:03.600
The history retains the old rightmost occurrence in a separate sphere. It's explicitly tagged as shifted out.

11:03.960 --> 11:05.300
Shifted out. That's amazing.

11:05.300 --> 11:08.940
It serves as the physical evidence of the bit that was pushed off the edge.

11:09.760 --> 11:16.800
So the ledger retains the forensic evidence to tell the two histories apart, even though the final visible output is identical.

11:17.160 --> 11:19.780
Yes. It resurrects the lost bits.

11:20.300 --> 11:26.260
It resurrects lost bits. It proves the model can reconstruct a physical machine while keeping the history intact.

11:26.720 --> 11:27.240
Precisely.

11:28.040 --> 11:32.880
So if we can model a single chip, how do we get to a programming language?

11:32.880 --> 11:36.120
And honestly, why does the text start with fourth of all things?

11:36.780 --> 11:39.780
Fourth is a fascinating starting point because of its architecture.

11:40.220 --> 11:44.600
It was developed in the 1970s for hardware that had extremely limited memory.

11:45.200 --> 11:45.840
Very old school.

11:46.120 --> 11:46.360
Very.

11:46.760 --> 11:49.920
It doesn't use named variables the way modern languages do.

11:50.060 --> 11:52.020
It is a stack-based language.

11:52.560 --> 11:54.260
Like a stack of plates at a buffet.

11:54.600 --> 11:54.980
Yes.

11:54.980 --> 12:00.700
You push numbers onto the top of the stack and operations pull numbers off the top.

12:01.040 --> 12:01.820
That is the structure.

12:02.480 --> 12:06.800
In fourth, programs are literally just histories of a stack evolving over time.

12:07.200 --> 12:07.460
Okay.

12:07.580 --> 12:09.400
So how does math work then?

12:09.400 --> 12:14.320
Well, if you want to add 2 and 3, you don't write an equation like 2 plus 3 equals x.

12:14.480 --> 12:19.420
You push 2 onto the stack, you push 3 onto the stack, then you call the plus operation.

12:19.860 --> 12:26.260
The plus operation pops the top two numbers, adds them, and pushes the result 5 back onto the stack.

12:26.840 --> 12:27.160
Okay.

12:27.300 --> 12:31.460
So in the SpherePop version, each number on that stack is a live sphere.

12:31.640 --> 12:31.920
Right.

12:31.920 --> 12:38.960
So when you call plus, it uses the POP primitive on the 3 and the 2 and uses the BIND primitive to create the 5.

12:39.800 --> 12:43.760
And it records the 3 and the 2 as dependencies for the 5.

12:43.940 --> 12:46.380
That is the exact mechanical translation.

12:47.100 --> 12:47.400
Okay.

12:47.820 --> 12:49.140
Let me push back on this, though.

12:49.320 --> 12:49.580
Sure.

12:49.940 --> 12:54.420
If I'm a programmer and I just care about my calculator giving me the answer 5,

12:54.920 --> 12:59.180
why do I care if the machine popped 3, then 2, or 2, then 3?

12:59.480 --> 13:00.660
I see what you're asking.

13:00.660 --> 13:02.680
I mean, the answer is 5 either way.

13:02.860 --> 13:05.800
Does the operand order really matter that much to the ledger?

13:06.300 --> 13:08.640
It matters immensely for the integrity of the history.

13:09.060 --> 13:11.360
You are thinking of addition, which is commutative.

13:11.560 --> 13:13.080
The order doesn't change the result.

13:13.260 --> 13:13.460
Right.

13:13.720 --> 13:16.460
But subtraction and division are not commutative.

13:16.820 --> 13:22.220
If you write 7, 2 minus in fourth, the system pushes 7, then pushes 2.

13:22.560 --> 13:25.180
So the stack has 2 on top, 7 underneath.

13:25.420 --> 13:25.580
Right.

13:25.960 --> 13:29.720
The minus operation physically must pop the 2 first.

13:29.720 --> 13:31.280
That is the right operand.

13:31.760 --> 13:34.480
Then it pops the 7, the left operand.

13:35.100 --> 13:35.560
Oh, I see.

13:36.060 --> 13:40.140
If the prototype popped them in the wrong order, it might accidentally pass your test for addition,

13:40.300 --> 13:42.620
but it would fail disastrously on subtraction.

13:43.040 --> 13:43.360
Right.

13:43.540 --> 13:46.480
Because 2 minus 7 is very different from 7 minus 2.

13:46.560 --> 13:46.960
Exactly.

13:47.480 --> 13:51.540
By popping them in the exact, rigid order the real silicon machine does,

13:51.860 --> 13:53.860
and recording that sequence in the dependencies,

13:54.500 --> 13:57.480
the SpherePop ledger can actually explain its own work.

13:57.480 --> 13:59.060
It's showing its math.

13:59.200 --> 13:59.460
Yes.

13:59.900 --> 14:02.340
If you ask the ledger where a result came from,

14:02.440 --> 14:06.500
it reads the dependencies in order and can literally print out 7 minus 2.

14:06.860 --> 14:09.340
It provides the strict provenance of the calculation.

14:09.740 --> 14:13.520
It proves the compiler's fidelity to the original machine's semantics.

14:13.940 --> 14:14.560
It really does.

14:14.940 --> 14:16.820
It's literally explaining its own math homework.

14:16.920 --> 14:17.680
That makes total sense.

14:18.100 --> 14:19.400
Now, what about the DUP command?

14:19.940 --> 14:21.300
Ah, duplication.

14:21.300 --> 14:22.760
Yeah, the tech spent some time on this.

14:23.100 --> 14:26.320
In fourth, DUP duplicates the top number on the stack.

14:26.960 --> 14:31.800
How does an immutable ledger handle duplication if you can't just copy a memory address?

14:32.200 --> 14:35.800
This touches on a really deep philosophical issue in computer science,

14:36.040 --> 14:37.520
identity versus value.

14:38.060 --> 14:38.380
Okay.

14:38.380 --> 14:42.940
If you have a 3 on the stack and you call DUP, you now have two 3s.

14:43.480 --> 14:47.680
But conceptually, is that two references pointing to a single value in memory?

14:48.060 --> 14:52.260
Or is it a value in a completely independent, newly manufactured copy?

14:52.700 --> 14:53.020
Right.

14:53.140 --> 14:55.560
Because in a normal computer today, to save space,

14:55.620 --> 14:59.860
it might just be two pointers aiming at the exact same physical transistor holding the number 3.

15:00.040 --> 15:00.460
Exactly.

15:01.020 --> 15:04.060
But in SpherePop, the ledger proves it is a new sphere.

15:04.640 --> 15:09.240
The DUP command uses the BIND primitive to create a brand new sphere with the payload 3.

15:09.600 --> 15:10.920
But how does it know it's a copy?

15:11.280 --> 15:14.960
It links it to the original 3 sphere as its dependency.

15:15.420 --> 15:20.380
So they are distinct physical objects in the ledger that can be consumed independently by future operations.

15:21.040 --> 15:25.080
But the history permanently shows that the second one was derived directly from the first.

15:25.200 --> 15:25.520
Yes.

15:25.920 --> 15:26.820
That's so elegant.

15:27.420 --> 15:28.540
What about logic, though?

15:28.540 --> 15:31.360
How do you do an if statement when you can't delete anything?

15:32.000 --> 15:32.700
Control flow is tricky.

15:32.980 --> 15:34.400
Yeah, like if I write a program that says,

15:34.920 --> 15:36.220
if the number is even, do A.

15:36.260 --> 15:37.280
If it's odd, do B.

15:37.600 --> 15:39.960
How does the ledger handle the path I don't take?

15:40.880 --> 15:43.680
This is where the REFUSE primitive does the heavy lifting.

15:44.400 --> 15:47.900
Flyxion models control flow as a physical manifestation of choice.

15:48.300 --> 15:49.160
Physical choice.

15:49.680 --> 15:53.260
When the program hits an if statement, it evaluates the condition.

15:53.820 --> 15:56.580
In a normal computer, the not-taken branch is just ignored.

15:56.580 --> 15:58.520
The processor jumps past it.

15:58.740 --> 16:00.060
It effectively never happened.

16:00.900 --> 16:05.300
But in our obsessive accountant universe, it has to document the alternative.

16:05.780 --> 16:06.200
Exactly.

16:06.760 --> 16:11.260
The system literally binds spheres for both potential branches as proposals.

16:11.940 --> 16:13.680
It materializes both futures.

16:14.220 --> 16:14.740
Both futures.

16:15.340 --> 16:15.660
Wow.

16:16.280 --> 16:20.700
Then it uses a parallel mechanism, pop A or refuse B.

16:20.700 --> 16:24.940
It pops the branch it is actually going to take, committing it to execution.

16:25.540 --> 16:25.960
And the other one?

16:26.580 --> 16:30.900
It refuses the branch it didn't take, recording a mandatory reason in the ledger.

16:31.160 --> 16:32.580
In this case, not-taken.

16:32.980 --> 16:36.160
The path not-taken is permanently recorded as a refusal.

16:36.560 --> 16:36.800
Yes.

16:37.320 --> 16:44.100
You could literally audit the ledger after the program runs and see exactly which alternate timelines the code considered but rejected.

16:44.440 --> 16:45.900
That is so robust.

16:46.120 --> 16:46.520
It is.

16:46.520 --> 16:48.560
And the same logic applies to errors.

16:49.120 --> 16:54.880
If you try to add numbers on an empty stack, it doesn't just crash, wipe its state, and dump an unhelpful error code.

16:55.220 --> 16:55.800
What does it do?

16:56.360 --> 17:04.820
It binds a proposal to add, immediately refuses it with the reason stack under flow, and then refuses the rest of the program with the reason abort.

17:05.180 --> 17:08.460
You get a perfect step-by-step autopsy report.

17:08.640 --> 17:10.360
A perfect autopsy report.

17:10.580 --> 17:12.040
That is incredible for debugging.

17:12.040 --> 17:15.620
But, you know, Forth is basically a calculator on steroids.

17:16.160 --> 17:17.980
Real software has variables that mutate.

17:18.060 --> 17:18.900
They change over time.

17:19.120 --> 17:19.320
They do.

17:19.680 --> 17:22.640
How do you change a variable if you can't erase the old one?

17:23.100 --> 17:27.180
To answer that, the text moves from Forth to a Lisp prototype.

17:27.440 --> 17:27.660
Right.

17:27.780 --> 17:28.200
Lisp.

17:28.640 --> 17:37.580
Before we get into how it mutates, for anyone who hasn't built a compiler lately, what exactly do we mean by environments and closures in Lisp?

17:37.980 --> 17:39.840
Think of an environment as a dictionary.

17:39.840 --> 17:43.420
It maps a variable name, a symbol, to a specific value.

17:43.960 --> 17:44.000
Okay.

17:44.160 --> 17:44.600
Dictionary.

17:45.280 --> 17:49.920
A closure is essentially a function that carries its environment around with it, like a backpack.

17:50.500 --> 17:56.700
So if you have a function that uses a variable X, the closure ensures it always knows what X is.

17:57.000 --> 17:57.980
Because it's in the backpack.

17:58.360 --> 17:58.820
Exactly.

17:59.100 --> 18:01.740
Even if it moves to a different part of the program, it has the backpack.

18:02.520 --> 18:02.820
Okay.

18:02.860 --> 18:07.020
So let's say I have a variable X in my backpack, and I set it to 10.

18:07.740 --> 18:08.360
That's a sphere.

18:08.360 --> 18:12.240
A binding sphere with the key X and the payload 10.

18:12.840 --> 18:14.460
Now, I want to mutate it.

18:14.940 --> 18:16.860
I write a line of code to set X to 20.

18:17.580 --> 18:22.000
Since the ledger is immutable, I cannot overwrite the physical sphere holding the 10.

18:22.360 --> 18:22.840
You cannot.

18:23.480 --> 18:26.700
So how does the ledger record the change without breaking the rules?

18:26.700 --> 18:30.900
Flyxion introduces a mechanism called mutation by supersession.

18:31.300 --> 18:31.900
Supersession.

18:32.080 --> 18:42.700
When you call the set command to change X to 20, the system uses the REFUSE primitive on the old sphere holding 10, giving it the specific reason superseded.

18:42.700 --> 18:51.540
Simultaneously, it uses the BIND primitive to create a new sphere holding 20 using the exact same key, X.

18:51.880 --> 18:55.440
And it records the old 10 sphere as its direct dependency.

18:55.440 --> 18:57.420
So the old value isn't gone.

18:57.540 --> 18:59.300
It's just marked as retired.

18:59.720 --> 19:02.240
And it explicitly points to its successor.

19:02.460 --> 19:04.740
Or rather, the successor points back to it.

19:04.940 --> 19:07.860
The history of a variable becomes a literal family tree.

19:08.320 --> 19:09.600
The text calls it a lineage.

19:10.000 --> 19:10.440
A lineage.

19:10.440 --> 19:20.340
If you mutate a variable a hundred times over the course of an hour, you can query its lineage and get a perfect list of every value it ever held, in chronological order.

19:20.600 --> 19:22.040
All linked together in the ledger.

19:22.180 --> 19:22.540
Exactly.

19:22.940 --> 19:24.260
That handles variables perfectly.

19:24.780 --> 19:28.000
But here is where the mechanics get really fascinating.

19:28.500 --> 19:32.460
The text details a revelation about recursion and tail calls.

19:32.740 --> 19:33.720
Yes, tail calls.

19:34.300 --> 19:38.820
In a normal computer, if a function calls itself over and over again, it crashes.

19:39.120 --> 19:39.320
Right.

19:39.320 --> 19:40.740
Why does that happen?

19:41.080 --> 19:42.700
And how does this ledger solve it?

19:43.040 --> 19:45.600
It crashes because of how traditional memory stacks work.

19:46.120 --> 19:52.900
Every time you call a function, the computer creates an activation frame in its memory stack to remember where to return to when the function finishes.

19:53.400 --> 19:53.640
Okay.

19:54.180 --> 20:00.440
A tail call is a specific situation where a function calls another function as its absolute last action before returning.

20:00.780 --> 20:01.060
Right.

20:01.580 --> 20:08.000
And if you have a deep recursive loop, like a function calling itself thousands of times, those activation frames just pile up.

20:08.000 --> 20:11.880
They pile up until the computer runs out of physical memory and crashes.

20:12.380 --> 20:13.720
And that's a stack overflow.

20:14.100 --> 20:15.320
That is a stack overflow.

20:16.080 --> 20:18.600
But SpherePop handles this completely differently.

20:19.200 --> 20:22.300
In the SpherePop Lisp, an activation is just a sphere.

20:22.760 --> 20:23.040
Okay.

20:23.040 --> 20:30.100
When the evaluator sees a tail call, it realizes it doesn't need to remember where to return to because there's no more work to do in the current function.

20:30.360 --> 20:31.580
So it doesn't need a new frame.

20:31.940 --> 20:32.180
Right.

20:32.700 --> 20:35.400
So it doesn't pile a new activation on top of the old one.

20:35.900 --> 20:39.920
Instead, it uses the POP primitive on the old activation sphere, committing it to history.

20:39.920 --> 20:43.880
Then it binds a new activation sphere for the next step of the loop.

20:44.480 --> 20:47.300
Let me use an analogy here to see if I'm visualizing this right.

20:47.500 --> 20:47.880
Go for it.

20:48.760 --> 20:50.000
It's like a relay race.

20:50.840 --> 20:57.740
In a normal computer, when a runner hands off the baton to the next person, they stay on the track and keep running alongside the new runner.

20:57.940 --> 20:58.200
Yes.

20:58.200 --> 21:04.880
Eventually, you have thousands of runners cluttering up the physical track until there's no room left, and the race just collapses.

21:05.500 --> 21:08.340
That is a very apt visualization of a stack overflow.

21:08.940 --> 21:09.960
The track is your RAM.

21:10.340 --> 21:20.800
But in the SpherePop relay race, the old runner hands off the baton, that's popping the old activation, and immediately vanishes from the active track into the history books.

21:21.100 --> 21:21.660
Exactly.

21:21.980 --> 21:24.820
They are no longer taking up physical space on the track.

21:25.080 --> 21:27.020
The memory bounding is flawless.

21:27.020 --> 21:32.260
The text notes a test where a Lisp loop runs 3,000 tail calls.

21:32.720 --> 21:33.440
3,000?

21:33.620 --> 21:40.760
Because of this supersession design, at the end of the loop, it finishes with exactly zero live call spheres taking up active space.

21:41.020 --> 21:41.460
Zero?

21:41.640 --> 21:41.840
Zero.

21:42.380 --> 21:42.900
Wow.

21:43.200 --> 21:47.100
The evaluator is just an infinite loop that commits the old and binds the new.

21:47.340 --> 21:53.560
It bounds the live space perfectly while retaining the entire 3,000-step journey in the immutable ledger.

21:53.560 --> 22:08.160
So, if you are a listener who has ever had a web browser crash from a memory leak or a video game crash to the desktop with a stack overflow error, you are seeing a failure of exactly what this Lisp tail call design elegantly solves.

22:08.840 --> 22:09.240
Absolutely.

22:09.240 --> 22:20.260
It proves that maintaining a perfect history doesn't inherently have to exhaust active resources, as long as the semantics of the language are reconstructed properly in the primitives.

22:20.260 --> 22:26.740
It separates the active working state from the historical record, which is a really profound architectural shift.

22:26.860 --> 22:27.240
It's incredible.

22:28.080 --> 22:30.800
Well, let's take a time machine to the 1980s.

22:31.160 --> 22:33.980
The next prototype in the text is Commodore BASIC.

22:34.300 --> 22:35.160
Ah, a classic.

22:35.160 --> 22:38.640
Why is this a different beast from Forth or Lisp?

22:39.040 --> 22:43.400
It's a completely different paradigm because of the constraints of the microcomputer era.

22:43.660 --> 22:50.960
In the 1980s, when you turned on a Commodore 64, there wasn't a complex operating system with file browsers sitting underneath your code.

22:51.060 --> 22:51.320
Right.

22:51.540 --> 22:56.840
The program store, the actual text of the code held in memory, is the operating system.

22:57.060 --> 22:57.960
I remember that feeling.

22:58.100 --> 23:02.500
You turn on the machine, and it just has a blue screen that says, ready, with a blinking cursor.

23:02.700 --> 23:02.900
Yep.

23:02.900 --> 23:04.800
And you just start typing lines of code.

23:05.140 --> 23:07.280
10, print hello, 20, go to 10.

23:07.680 --> 23:09.200
And you interact with it directly.

23:09.600 --> 23:15.220
In this SpherePop prototype, the lines of code themselves are modeled as the live spheres.

23:15.460 --> 23:15.720
Okay.

23:15.900 --> 23:16.640
The lines themselves.

23:16.840 --> 23:22.160
A line has its line number as its key, and the text string as its payload.

23:22.900 --> 23:28.740
So when I type 10, print, hello, the system binds a sphere with key 10.

23:29.000 --> 23:29.440
Exactly.

23:29.440 --> 23:33.760
What happens if I make a typo, and I want to fix it by typing a new line 10?

23:34.060 --> 23:37.420
Like I type 10, print, world, and hit enter.

23:37.920 --> 23:39.600
Does it just overwrite the memory address?

23:39.980 --> 23:46.260
No, it uses the exact same mutation by supersession we just discussed with Lisp variables.

23:46.380 --> 23:47.140
Oh, of course.

23:47.140 --> 23:51.800
The old line 10 sphere is refused with the reason replaced.

23:52.100 --> 23:56.000
And the new line 10 is bound, linked directly to the old one.

23:56.620 --> 23:57.660
And if I delete it?

23:57.880 --> 24:05.300
If you type just the number 10 and hit enter, which is how you delete a line in BASIC, it refuses the sphere with the reason deleted.

24:05.300 --> 24:06.940
Wait a minute.

24:08.060 --> 24:08.380
Yeah.

24:08.540 --> 24:10.520
Because editing is supersession.

24:11.120 --> 24:13.940
The old text of line 10 isn't lost when I replace it.

24:14.000 --> 24:17.200
It's still sitting in the ledger as a refused sphere with its successor.

24:17.500 --> 24:20.040
It remains queryable in the history.

24:21.040 --> 24:27.840
The SpherePop ledger accidentally gave 1982 Commodore BASIC a perfect modern version control system.

24:27.920 --> 24:28.300
It did.

24:28.300 --> 24:31.620
It's like having Git baked into a Commodore 64 at the hardware level.

24:32.340 --> 24:36.520
Every single edit, every typo, every deleted line is permanently preserved in the ledger.

24:36.760 --> 24:41.840
You could literally reconstruct every version of your program that existed during a coding session.

24:42.060 --> 24:43.840
What would that have meant for a programmer back then?

24:44.360 --> 24:47.920
It would have eliminated the concept of losing work to a bad edit.

24:48.680 --> 24:52.980
Flyxion actually points out that they didn't set out to add version control as a feature.

24:52.980 --> 24:58.180
It just naturally falls out of the fact that the program store is made of spheres in an immutable ledger.

24:58.300 --> 24:59.040
That is wild.

24:59.180 --> 25:00.280
It just happens naturally.

25:00.480 --> 25:00.640
Yeah.

25:01.200 --> 25:03.640
And the text goes into detail about the load command, too.

25:04.260 --> 25:11.160
Back in the day, you'd type load, quote, dollar sign, unquote, comma, eight to read the floppy disk directory.

25:11.300 --> 25:11.520
Right.

25:12.060 --> 25:15.000
How does a ledger model loading a program from a disk?

25:15.520 --> 25:17.900
The disk is modeled as a family of file spheres.

25:18.140 --> 25:23.880
When you issue a load command, the prototype models the exact documented behavior of the original machine.

25:24.460 --> 25:26.440
It searches the disk device for the file name.

25:26.740 --> 25:26.780
Okay.

25:26.780 --> 25:28.980
But here is the critical mechanism.

25:29.240 --> 25:32.500
When it loads a new program into memory, it must clear the old one.

25:32.620 --> 25:32.840
Right.

25:32.920 --> 25:34.080
Because memory is so limited.

25:34.660 --> 25:36.180
How does it clear it without deleting it?

25:36.480 --> 25:44.800
It uses the REFUSE primitive on every single live line of the currently running program with the documented reason replaced by load.

25:45.020 --> 25:45.280
Wow.

25:45.420 --> 25:48.740
Then it binds all the new lines from the file sphere into the active world.

25:48.740 --> 25:52.420
So loading a new program refuses every line of the old program.

25:52.620 --> 25:52.940
Yes.

25:53.160 --> 25:58.260
And loading the dollar sign reads the physical disk directory and turns it into a runnable program.

25:58.900 --> 26:03.400
Does that mean the directory of the disk and the code itself are literally the exact same kind of object?

26:03.400 --> 26:06.500
They are indistinguishable to the kernel.

26:06.500 --> 26:08.340
They are both just spheres.

26:08.560 --> 26:09.260
That's crazy.

26:09.620 --> 26:16.380
A program that is a listing of a disk's directory is just an ordinary stored program in memory as far as the system is concerned.

26:16.380 --> 26:26.240
If loading a program is just taking a name you typed, searching a disk for it, and putting it in memory, that sounds exactly like what happens when I open my modern terminal today.

26:26.380 --> 26:26.920
It is.

26:27.140 --> 26:32.100
Which brings us to shells, search paths, and package indexes.

26:32.380 --> 26:34.720
It is the exact same underlying problem.

26:35.320 --> 26:42.680
Flyxion groups Bash, the Windows command prompt, and package managers together because a shell is primarily a loading language.

26:42.900 --> 26:43.660
A loading language.

26:43.660 --> 26:49.120
Its main job is taking a name you typed, resolving it via an ordered search, and loading the result.

26:49.840 --> 26:56.740
So when I open my terminal and type ls to list my files, my computer doesn't just magically know what ls is or where it lives.

26:56.820 --> 26:58.580
It has to go on the scavenger hunt.

26:58.760 --> 26:59.180
It does.

26:59.560 --> 27:04.560
It searches a list of directories defined in your system's path variable in order.

27:05.020 --> 27:07.100
The path is just a long string of text.

27:07.240 --> 27:07.400
Okay.

27:07.400 --> 27:13.820
The SpherePop prototype implements this search function, and every single directory it checks is recorded as a sphere.

27:14.000 --> 27:14.840
A probe sphere.

27:15.100 --> 27:15.520
A probe.

27:15.880 --> 27:19.380
A probe that misses is refused with the reason missing.

27:19.600 --> 27:20.060
Makes sense.

27:20.440 --> 27:26.500
A probe that finds a file but realizes you don't have permission to execute it is refused with the reason no exec.

27:26.920 --> 27:27.340
Oh, wow.

27:27.340 --> 27:32.980
And the probe that finally finds the right executable program is popped and returned as a hit.

27:33.080 --> 27:36.180
So the ledger holds a complete record of the scavenger hunt.

27:36.400 --> 27:36.880
Exactly.

27:37.560 --> 27:40.800
The text gives a great example of Bash caching these lookups.

27:41.220 --> 27:44.620
What happens the first time you type ls versus the second time?

27:44.620 --> 27:50.360
The first time you type ls, the ledger shows the shell probing, say, six different directories.

27:50.640 --> 27:56.180
It misses on the first five, resulting in five refused spheres, and hits on the sixth.

27:56.280 --> 27:56.520
Okay.

27:57.040 --> 28:01.100
The shell then caches that successful location in memory so it doesn't have to search again.

28:01.560 --> 28:04.000
Wait, the paper mentions a hash sphere here.

28:04.520 --> 28:05.540
What exactly is that?

28:05.580 --> 28:06.600
Is it cryptographic?

28:07.180 --> 28:12.660
In this context, it's a cached location record that is hashed to the state of the path.

28:12.780 --> 28:13.860
It's essentially a bookmark.

28:13.860 --> 28:14.480
A bookmark.

28:14.980 --> 28:15.160
Right.

28:15.740 --> 28:20.200
The second time you type ls, the shell checks for a hash sphere first.

28:20.480 --> 28:24.960
It finds it, verifies the path hasn't changed, and executes the program immediately.

28:25.540 --> 28:26.120
And the ledger?

28:26.480 --> 28:30.400
The ledger shows exactly zero directory probes for that second run.

28:30.820 --> 28:34.360
It's proof in the immutable ledger of why the second command ran faster.

28:34.600 --> 28:34.940
Yes.

28:35.540 --> 28:39.260
And what happens if I change my path variable by installing a new program?

28:39.260 --> 28:43.980
The prototype refuses every single hash sphere with the reason that the path changed.

28:43.980 --> 28:45.520
It invalidates the cache.

28:45.860 --> 28:46.520
So it starts over.

28:47.100 --> 28:51.020
It forces the shell to start the scavenger hunt from scratch the next time you type a command.

28:51.380 --> 28:55.660
This totally explains that annoying experience where you move a program on your computer,

28:55.660 --> 28:59.760
but the terminal keeps trying to run it from the old location until you told it to forget the cache.

28:59.760 --> 29:00.320
Yes.

29:00.320 --> 29:04.960
The ledger literally makes that invisible system behavior visible.

29:05.180 --> 29:05.620
It does.

29:05.940 --> 29:07.920
And different shells handle this search differently.

29:08.500 --> 29:12.200
Windows command prompt, for instance, searches the current directory first,

29:12.200 --> 29:19.000
and then tries a list of file extensions, like .com, .exe, .bat, for every single directory in the path.

29:19.200 --> 29:19.460
Right.

29:19.800 --> 29:24.840
If you type a command that doesn't exist, the ledger will show dozens of refused probes

29:24.840 --> 29:29.500
as it frantically checks every single extension in every single folder before giving up.

29:29.540 --> 29:30.700
It's a very noisy search.

29:31.160 --> 29:34.600
And then there's WSL, the Windows subsystem for Linux.

29:34.600 --> 29:37.820
This is where you run Linux inside Windows.

29:38.720 --> 29:43.300
How does a sphere cross a file system boundary from Linux to Windows?

29:43.840 --> 29:48.020
The prototype builds this as two distinct namespaces residing in one world,

29:48.140 --> 29:50.460
a Windows namespace and a Linux namespace.

29:50.580 --> 29:50.720
Okay.

29:51.060 --> 29:55.080
If you type notepad.exe while inside the Linux subsystem,

29:55.620 --> 29:58.840
the ledger shows the shell probing all the Linux directories first.

29:59.080 --> 30:00.240
It misses on all of them.

30:00.340 --> 30:00.600
Obviously.

30:00.600 --> 30:05.040
Then the kernel translates the Linux path string into a Windows mount point.

30:05.320 --> 30:07.460
So it rewrites the path as a new sphere.

30:07.780 --> 30:08.000
Yes.

30:08.180 --> 30:10.200
It binds a translated proxy sphere.

30:10.540 --> 30:15.080
It then continues the search in the Windows namespace and finally hits the Windows system directory.

30:15.360 --> 30:17.020
And the ledger tracks all of that.

30:17.440 --> 30:20.980
The ledger perfectly tracks the command crossing the operating system boundary,

30:21.320 --> 30:24.100
recording the translation step as part of the dependency chain.

30:24.520 --> 30:25.760
That is wild.

30:25.940 --> 30:26.180
Mm-hmm.

30:26.560 --> 30:27.500
What about BusyBox?

30:27.500 --> 30:30.340
I know that's used in a lot of embedded systems and routers.

30:30.880 --> 30:32.520
BusyBox is the ultimate shortcut.

30:33.020 --> 30:39.460
It implements dozens of small programs like cat, echo, and ls inside a single executable binary file.

30:39.560 --> 30:40.020
Only one.

30:40.400 --> 30:44.460
The file system just has a bunch of symbolic links pointing to that one file.

30:44.700 --> 30:49.040
When you type ls, the shell resolves the link, finds the BusyBox executable,

30:49.820 --> 30:52.020
and passes the name ls to it as an argument.

30:52.020 --> 30:57.160
So instead of a deep directory search, it just delegates the problem to the program itself?

30:57.540 --> 30:58.020
Exactly.

30:58.400 --> 30:58.600
Okay.

30:58.620 --> 31:02.920
For the structural analysis, BusyBox has the shallowest search of all the shells,

31:03.220 --> 31:05.940
but the most indirection between the name and the code.

31:06.280 --> 31:06.740
Okay.

31:06.880 --> 31:09.140
So shells find programs on your local computer.

31:09.140 --> 31:12.100
But what about finding programs on the internet?

31:12.480 --> 31:13.620
That's where it gets complicated.

31:13.820 --> 31:14.100
Right.

31:14.400 --> 31:16.660
This brings us to package indexes.

31:17.060 --> 31:21.380
If you're a developer, you use tools like npm or pip to download code libraries.

31:22.220 --> 31:26.140
And this introduces a concept that strikes fear into the hearts of all programmers.

31:27.420 --> 31:28.180
Dependency hell.

31:28.180 --> 31:33.440
The package manager chapters are where the ordered search gets exponentially more complex.

31:34.040 --> 31:36.980
Because now, you aren't just finding a file on your hard drive,

31:37.080 --> 31:40.200
you are resolving mathematical constraints over the internet.

31:40.480 --> 31:41.040
Constraints.

31:41.520 --> 31:44.520
Package A might require version 2 of package C,

31:44.900 --> 31:47.760
but package B might require version 3 of package C.

31:47.980 --> 31:51.300
This is the infamous diamond dependency problem.

31:51.560 --> 31:51.920
Yes.

31:52.640 --> 31:56.280
And Flyxion implements two different solutions to this in the prototype.

31:56.280 --> 31:59.780
Flat resolution and nested resolution.

32:00.720 --> 32:05.080
Before we get into the solutions, what is lexical scope in this context?

32:05.540 --> 32:08.760
The paper relies on it heavily for the nested resolution.

32:09.460 --> 32:13.660
Lexical scope is essentially the boundary of visibility for a piece of code.

32:13.980 --> 32:18.820
If a variable or a package is within your lexical scope, you can see it and use it.

32:19.380 --> 32:20.260
And if it's not?

32:20.600 --> 32:23.180
If it is outside, it's invisible to you.

32:23.180 --> 32:28.580
It's a way of walling off information so different parts of a program don't accidentally interfere with each other.

32:29.340 --> 32:34.560
Okay, keeping that wall in mind, how does flat resolution work in the SpherePop ledger?

32:35.340 --> 32:41.600
Flat resolution means you can only have exactly one version of any package installed globally.

32:42.160 --> 32:42.680
No walls.

32:43.040 --> 32:43.860
No walls at all.

32:43.860 --> 32:46.760
The solver does a depth-first search.

32:47.200 --> 32:52.280
It picks a version of a package, binds a decision sphere, and keeps going.

32:52.680 --> 32:57.780
If it hits a conflict, like finding out later that another package needs a different version, it hits a wall.

32:58.320 --> 33:02.520
And because it's SpherePop, it can't just silently erase its mistake and try again.

33:02.620 --> 33:02.960
Exactly.

33:03.320 --> 33:08.200
It uses the REFUSE primitive on that decision sphere, recording the reason backtrack.

33:08.340 --> 33:09.000
Oh, backtrack.

33:09.000 --> 33:11.900
It goes back up the decision tree and tries a different version.

33:12.860 --> 33:19.940
If it can't find any combination that satisfies all the rules, it refuses the entire installation attempt with the reason unsatisfiable.

33:20.460 --> 33:22.000
Let me try an analogy here to ground this.

33:22.980 --> 33:31.400
Flat resolution is like trying to plan a wedding dinner where every single guest must agree on one single set menu for the entire room.

33:31.480 --> 33:32.180
I like this.

33:32.180 --> 33:39.560
If Uncle Bob demands vegan, but Aunt Wynne did demand steak, and they absolutely refuse to compromise, you hit a conflict.

33:40.460 --> 33:45.560
You backtrack, you try to propose fish, they reject it, eventually you just cancel the dinner.

33:45.740 --> 33:46.620
It's a refused attempt.

33:47.200 --> 33:50.400
That is an excellent way to visualize the rigidity of it.

33:50.640 --> 33:57.160
It's strict, it ensures everyone is eating the exact same thing, but it fails very easily when constraints clash.

33:57.160 --> 34:01.940
So what is nested resolution, and how does lexical scope fix the dinner?

34:02.560 --> 34:11.220
Nested resolution uses lexical scope to solve the diamond problem by allowing multiple versions of the same package to exist simultaneously, walled off from each other.

34:11.320 --> 34:11.720
Walled off.

34:11.960 --> 34:16.920
When package A asks for package C, it gets its own private copy of version 2.

34:17.500 --> 34:21.740
When package B asks for package C, it gets its own private copy of version 3.

34:21.740 --> 34:23.940
So, back to the wedding dinner.

34:24.360 --> 34:27.620
Nested resolution is just letting everyone order their own Uber Eats to the table.

34:27.800 --> 34:28.460
Pretty much.

34:28.800 --> 34:30.840
Uncle Bob gets his vegan meal in his own bag.

34:31.280 --> 34:33.040
Aunt Linda gets her steak in her own bag.

34:33.380 --> 34:34.500
The dinner succeeds.

34:35.080 --> 34:35.800
Everyone is eating.

34:36.300 --> 34:39.000
The resolution succeeds where the flat policy failed.

34:39.340 --> 34:42.960
However, there is a severe architectural trade-off.

34:43.240 --> 34:44.700
The table gets incredibly cluttered.

34:44.780 --> 34:45.040
Mm-hmm.

34:45.260 --> 34:47.320
You've got 50 different Uber Eats bags everywhere.

34:47.940 --> 34:49.600
It's massive memory bloat.

34:49.800 --> 34:50.160
Yes.

34:50.160 --> 34:55.420
And what happens if Uncle Bob tries to share his vegan food with Aunt Linda, but she's expecting steak?

34:55.680 --> 34:57.720
That is exactly the technical danger.

34:57.980 --> 35:01.460
The installation now contains multiple copies of the same library.

35:01.920 --> 35:10.880
And if objects created by version 2 are passed into a function expecting version 3, the system might crash in unpredictable ways because they have different internal structures.

35:11.160 --> 35:11.380
Wow.

35:11.980 --> 35:14.000
The ledger perfectly records this trade-off.

35:14.120 --> 35:19.160
In the flat policy, you see backtracked decision spheres and a lean single-version installation.

35:19.160 --> 35:20.540
And in the nested policy?

35:21.080 --> 35:29.120
You see no backtracking, but you see nested spheres keyed by their specific parent packages, resulting in a much larger live sphere count.

35:29.340 --> 35:32.140
This is literally the mechanics of dependency health.

35:32.940 --> 35:38.900
It's the exact reason why updating one single app on your smartphone sometimes randomly breaks another app that seemed completely unrelated.

35:38.900 --> 35:39.500
Exactly.

35:39.860 --> 35:41.900
They were fighting over the same menu at the wedding dinner.

35:42.240 --> 35:45.980
And Flyxion's ledger shows that this is not just a theoretical annoyance.

35:46.380 --> 35:49.800
It is a structural reality of how names are resolved and bound in memory.

35:49.800 --> 35:57.120
So package managers can deliver the code, but bringing third-party code into your environment is dangerous.

35:57.780 --> 36:00.460
It can literally rewrite the rules of your own language.

36:01.040 --> 36:01.460
It can.

36:01.940 --> 36:06.020
How does an immutable ledger handle code that alters its own reality?

36:06.480 --> 36:10.620
This brings us to modules, macros, and building domain-specific languages.

36:10.620 --> 36:14.560
To explore this, the text moves to a Racket layer.

36:15.160 --> 36:19.300
Racket is a language famous for letting developers build other languages inside of it.

36:19.540 --> 36:20.360
Inception style.

36:20.480 --> 36:20.740
Yes.

36:21.700 --> 36:26.720
Flyxion identifies two distinct ways a domain-specific language changes a program.

36:27.160 --> 36:28.800
Module languages versus macros.

36:29.140 --> 36:30.660
Let's start with module languages.

36:31.160 --> 36:32.620
How does that look in the ledger?

36:33.020 --> 36:36.120
A module language acts as an initial import set.

36:36.120 --> 36:38.880
Let's say you import a module called saturating.

36:39.400 --> 36:40.580
That changes how math works.

36:41.180 --> 36:44.780
It cramps addition at a certain ceiling so numbers can't go infinitely high.

36:45.180 --> 36:45.220
Okay.

36:45.780 --> 36:52.640
When a module declares saturating as its language, it imports the special addition rule before it even reads the rest of your code.

36:52.900 --> 36:57.600
So it fundamentally changes the definition of the plus sign for the whole file.

36:57.980 --> 36:59.400
It redefines reality.

36:59.720 --> 37:00.000
Yes.

37:00.340 --> 37:02.300
And the ledger shows this precisely.

37:02.300 --> 37:08.160
Every name that the file gets from its base language is recorded as a lang import sphere.

37:08.700 --> 37:09.060
Lang import.

37:09.200 --> 37:11.740
It proves that the module language is not a metaphor.

37:12.520 --> 37:16.860
It is a literal set of spheres injected into the scope before evaluation begins.

37:17.100 --> 37:17.400
Okay.

37:17.660 --> 37:19.180
So what about macros?

37:19.880 --> 37:21.560
How are they different from a module language?

37:22.040 --> 37:24.720
A macro doesn't change the underlying language environment.

37:25.080 --> 37:28.840
It adds new syntax by rewriting your code before it gets evaluated.

37:29.200 --> 37:29.760
Rewriting it.

37:29.760 --> 37:35.320
The text gives an example of a macro for a finite state machine, like a traffic light.

37:36.160 --> 37:38.020
You write a clean, simple table of states.

37:38.700 --> 37:40.620
Green, yellow, red.

37:40.860 --> 37:41.060
Right.

37:41.340 --> 37:47.820
The macro reads that simple table and secretly expands it into complex table-walking function code behind the scenes.

37:48.180 --> 37:51.440
It's basically a glorified copy and paste text replacement tool.

37:52.340 --> 37:54.560
And how does the ledger record a macro?

37:54.560 --> 37:59.500
It shows up as a regular import sphere, not a lang import.

37:59.740 --> 38:00.100
Ah.

38:00.720 --> 38:05.180
The file simply imported a macro rule from another module and then applied it.

38:05.680 --> 38:13.980
The ledger shows that the module language fundamentally changes existing names, while the macro just adds new syntax without changing the old names.

38:13.980 --> 38:16.880
That distinction is so clear when you look at the spheres.

38:17.460 --> 38:20.660
But what happens if a module I depend on gets updated behind my back?

38:21.060 --> 38:27.060
Like, I'm using version 1 of the traffic light macro, but the author upstates it to version 2 on the remote server.

38:27.460 --> 38:29.340
Flyxion calls this staleness.

38:29.860 --> 38:30.340
Staleness.

38:30.340 --> 38:33.060
And the prototype models it beautifully.

38:33.600 --> 38:41.560
If the traffic light module is redeclared and updated, the prototype refuses the old module sphere and its old export spheres.

38:41.700 --> 38:42.940
So the old version is retired.

38:43.320 --> 38:43.560
Yes.

38:43.880 --> 38:50.240
But the importers, your code that is currently running, still hold the import spheres they were originally given.

38:50.940 --> 38:51.500
Wait, really?

38:52.000 --> 38:56.500
Those spheres are still linked to the old, refused definitions as dependencies.

38:56.500 --> 39:02.600
Oh, wow. So my code keeps running on the old logic because it's holding a permanent receipt for the old version.

39:03.140 --> 39:03.460
Exactly.

39:04.300 --> 39:10.720
Your code won't see the new definitions until your module is explicitly reloaded and reacquires the dependencies afresh.

39:11.140 --> 39:15.320
It perfectly models what happens when a dependency upgrades underneath you without you knowing.

39:15.800 --> 39:21.160
The ledger explains exactly why your code hasn't broken yet or why it suddenly behaves differently after a reboot.

39:21.560 --> 39:23.720
It's all just forensic accounting of dependencies.

39:23.720 --> 39:25.740
We've looked at all these isolated systems.

39:25.960 --> 39:28.600
We've looked at Forth, Lisp, Basic, Bash, and Racket packages.

39:29.120 --> 39:30.920
What happens when you mash them together?

39:31.480 --> 39:32.680
This is the big finale.

39:33.460 --> 39:39.280
What happens when Flyxion forces all these wildly different universes into the exact same ledger at the exact same time?

39:39.860 --> 39:42.540
This is the ultimate test of the SpherePop model.

39:43.420 --> 39:49.660
Flyxion runs a Racket call, a BASIC program, a Bash session, and a Forth program simultaneously,

39:50.280 --> 39:52.940
interleaving their operations in one single shared ledger.

39:53.720 --> 39:54.820
That sounds like absolute chaos.

39:55.840 --> 39:58.300
How do they not overwrite each other or steal each other's data?

39:58.640 --> 40:02.540
It's like trying to play a game of chess and a game of checkers on the exact same board at the same time.

40:02.620 --> 40:03.020
Exactly.

40:03.920 --> 40:08.260
Unless the pieces are strictly color-coded and the players only touch their specific colors,

40:08.680 --> 40:10.640
somebody is going to try to jump a knight with a checker.

40:10.980 --> 40:13.140
That's a perfect analogy.

40:13.380 --> 40:13.760
That's a good.

40:13.760 --> 40:17.520
And during early testing, Flyxion found exactly that kind of collision.

40:17.980 --> 40:18.260
Oh, really?

40:18.720 --> 40:25.460
If Forth output was tagged merely as out and BASIC output was tagged out, the languages would collide.

40:25.960 --> 40:29.280
A rule meant for Forth would accidentally scoop up a BASIC sphere.

40:29.920 --> 40:30.320
Yikes.

40:30.720 --> 40:33.480
The repair was to strictly namespace the tags.

40:33.980 --> 40:36.320
Forth variables are tagged Forth.var.

40:36.740 --> 40:38.720
Basic variables are Basic.var.

40:39.380 --> 40:39.660
Okay.

40:39.780 --> 40:40.300
That makes sense.

40:40.300 --> 40:43.960
Once this rule is in place, because the four primitives are strictly isolated,

40:44.160 --> 40:48.060
operations from different languages naming disjoint spheres perfectly commute.

40:48.220 --> 40:49.560
They don't interfere with each other.

40:49.900 --> 40:53.520
So they can coexist on the same board, but can they talk to each other?

40:53.900 --> 40:54.300
Yes.

40:54.720 --> 40:55.640
And this is the breakthrough.

40:55.860 --> 40:56.040
Okay.

40:56.260 --> 41:00.120
Because they share the underlying primitives, data can cross boundaries.

41:00.460 --> 41:00.700
Oh.

41:00.700 --> 41:06.200
The text describes a test where a Racket program calculates a sum, binds it as a sphere with

41:06.200 --> 41:10.420
a payload, and passes that exact sphere to the Forth stack.

41:10.800 --> 41:13.460
The Forth program then duplicates it and multiplies it.

41:13.740 --> 41:13.960
Wait.

41:14.620 --> 41:21.620
A functional Racket program calculates a number, hands the literal memory sphere to a 1970s stack-based

41:21.620 --> 41:25.780
Forth program, and the Forth program just naturally crunches it.

41:26.180 --> 41:26.560
Yes.

41:26.720 --> 41:27.260
That's incredible.

41:27.260 --> 41:31.460
Because they share no data representation other than a payload inside a sphere.

41:31.740 --> 41:32.940
That is incredible.

41:33.520 --> 41:36.960
The interface between the languages is the interface of the kernel itself.

41:37.540 --> 41:43.360
It proves that every language in this model can, in principle, be coupled to every other language seamlessly.

41:43.660 --> 41:45.200
That is mind-bending.

41:45.660 --> 41:48.700
But all of this history-keeping has to come with a cost, right?

41:49.200 --> 41:54.200
If we never delete anything and we track every variable mutation and directory probe, memory

41:54.200 --> 41:54.900
must fill up.

41:55.360 --> 41:55.940
It does.

41:55.940 --> 42:01.140
And Flyxion provides a measured profile, a comparative structural analysis of the workloads.

42:01.680 --> 42:05.580
By counting the spheres, we can see the true cost of different programming paradigms.

42:06.220 --> 42:07.060
What do the numbers look like?

42:07.540 --> 42:15.080
For a Forth program calculating a factorial, it binds 90 spheres, pops 78, refuses 10, and

42:15.080 --> 42:19.060
leaves only 2 live spheres at the end, the result and the remaining stack cell.

42:19.060 --> 42:24.060
So it's incredibly efficient because it consumes operands immediately as it uses them.

42:24.260 --> 42:24.760
Very lean.

42:25.220 --> 42:25.900
What about Lisp?

42:26.540 --> 42:35.100
A Lisp program calculating a Fibonacci number binds over 1,400 spheres and leaves 985 live spheres

42:35.100 --> 42:35.640
at the end.

42:36.920 --> 42:37.320
985?

42:37.320 --> 42:38.620
Why so many?

42:38.620 --> 42:38.700
Why so many?

42:38.700 --> 42:43.540
Because Lisp keeps environments and closures alive for as long as any binding refers to

42:43.540 --> 42:43.700
them.

42:43.700 --> 42:46.640
It maintains the physical history of its nested scopes.

42:46.640 --> 42:52.720
So whenever you wonder why software uses so much RAM today compared to the 80s, this structural

42:52.720 --> 42:54.480
analysis literally proves it.

42:55.180 --> 43:01.160
Maintaining the history of closures and environments in modern languages has an explicit, countable

43:01.160 --> 43:02.580
cost in memory spheres.

43:02.580 --> 43:04.540
And BASIC is even wilder.

43:05.100 --> 43:12.680
A simple BASIC loop generating a sum binds 207 spheres and generates 199 refusals for every

43:12.680 --> 43:13.480
one pop.

43:13.700 --> 43:15.560
Why on earth are there so many refusals?

43:16.120 --> 43:20.560
Because a BASIC loop supersedes its loop variables on every single pass.

43:20.680 --> 43:21.260
Think about it.

43:21.600 --> 43:22.220
I equals 1.

43:22.820 --> 43:23.400
Supersede it.

43:23.820 --> 43:24.540
I equals 2.

43:25.140 --> 43:25.640
Supersede it.

43:26.100 --> 43:26.820
I equals 3.

43:27.180 --> 43:27.540
Oh.

43:27.720 --> 43:30.720
Almost nothing is formally committed or popped.

43:30.720 --> 43:36.100
It's all just variables aggressively replacing themselves and leaving a massive trail of refused

43:36.100 --> 43:37.020
history in the ledger.

43:37.600 --> 43:41.060
The text introduces some terminology from substructural logic here.

43:41.740 --> 43:43.200
Weakening and contraction.

43:43.780 --> 43:47.340
What do those mean in the context of these spheres and how do they help us understand the

43:47.340 --> 43:47.660
cost?

43:48.060 --> 43:51.140
This is how we audit what a language is actually doing with data.

43:51.920 --> 43:53.880
Weakening means throwing things away.

43:54.440 --> 43:58.520
A refusal of a sphere that never contributed to a final result is a weakening.

43:58.520 --> 44:01.320
Like the directory probes in the shell that missed.

44:02.200 --> 44:05.460
Or the alternative branch of an if statement that wasn't taken.

44:05.960 --> 44:06.220
Yes.

44:07.120 --> 44:10.020
Contraction, on the other hand, means using things twice.

44:10.680 --> 44:16.100
The Forth DUP command is a contraction because it copies a sphere, allowing one value to be

44:16.100 --> 44:16.620
used twice.

44:17.020 --> 44:17.520
And Lisp.

44:18.060 --> 44:22.940
Reading a Lisp variable is also a contraction because you read it, but the binding stays live

44:22.940 --> 44:24.760
in the environment to be read again later.

44:25.520 --> 44:26.700
Synthesizing all of this?

44:27.160 --> 44:30.920
This SpherePop framework isn't just a quirky emulator for old code.

44:31.260 --> 44:33.680
It's a literal x-ray machine for software.

44:33.880 --> 44:35.060
That's a great way to look at it.

44:35.440 --> 44:41.900
By translating a program into bind, pop, refuse, and collapse, it strips away the syntax and

44:41.900 --> 44:46.920
shows us the skeletal structure of how a language handles data, choices, and memory.

44:46.920 --> 44:51.560
It makes the invisible rules of computation visible, countable, and checkable.

44:52.140 --> 44:53.680
It shows us the true cost of history.

44:54.100 --> 44:55.480
That is a brilliant synthesis.

44:56.060 --> 44:58.240
You are seeing the physical mechanics of logic.

44:58.620 --> 44:59.960
What an absolute journey.

45:00.640 --> 45:05.460
We are gone from a simple 4-bit shift register taking on a hardware clock edge, up through

45:05.460 --> 45:12.460
fourth stacks, Lisp tail calls, Commodore 64 program stores, Bash directory probes, package

45:12.460 --> 45:19.020
manager dependency hell, and finally a multi-language ecosystem, all unified by four simple primitives

45:19.020 --> 45:20.680
in an indestructible ledger.

45:21.160 --> 45:22.860
It is a profound proof of concept.

45:23.120 --> 45:23.700
It really is.

45:24.140 --> 45:30.580
The text mentions open problems at the end, including concurrency and first-class continuations.

45:30.940 --> 45:33.600
Before we wrap, what is a first-class continuation?

45:34.060 --> 45:39.260
Very simply, a first-class continuation is the ability to save the exact execution state of

45:39.260 --> 45:43.860
a program at a specific moment, and return to it later, much like a save state in a video game.

45:43.960 --> 45:44.900
Okay, I know what that is.

45:45.940 --> 45:50.740
Flyxion leaves it as an open problem how to elegantly model that kind of time travel

45:50.740 --> 45:52.980
in a strictly append-only history.

45:53.240 --> 45:54.780
That is fascinating.

45:55.660 --> 45:59.040
But to end this deep dive, I want to circle back to our opening thought.

45:59.600 --> 46:04.500
We talked about how traditional computers are amnesiacs, wiping their memory to give us

46:04.500 --> 46:05.240
a clean answer.

46:05.240 --> 46:09.120
The SpherePop ledger fixes that by keeping the receipts.

46:09.940 --> 46:13.040
It got me thinking about something even bigger outside of this paper.

46:13.520 --> 46:13.880
What's that?

46:14.400 --> 46:19.380
If SpherePop can perfectly track the history, the dependencies, and the discarded choices

46:19.380 --> 46:25.000
of computer code, imagine applying this immutable, traceable ledger to artificial intelligence

46:25.000 --> 46:25.860
decision-making.

46:26.520 --> 46:27.140
Oh, wow.

46:27.520 --> 46:29.280
Right now, AI is a black box.

46:29.380 --> 46:31.560
It gives us an answer, and we don't really know how it got there.

46:31.560 --> 46:38.260
But if an AI makes a choice, what if we could query its SpherePop ledger to see exactly which

46:38.260 --> 46:40.720
data points it popped and committed to?

46:41.000 --> 46:42.060
And which ones it refused.

46:42.200 --> 46:42.480
Yes.

46:42.880 --> 46:47.340
What if we could see which alternatives it refused, complete with documented reasons for

46:47.340 --> 46:48.480
why it rejected them?

46:48.960 --> 46:50.680
We wouldn't just have smart machines.

46:50.920 --> 46:53.060
We'd have perfectly accountable ones.

46:53.440 --> 46:55.860
A completely auditable mind.

46:56.080 --> 46:57.520
That is something to mull over.

46:57.720 --> 46:58.360
It really is.

46:58.360 --> 47:00.980
Thank you so much for joining us on this deep dive.

47:01.540 --> 47:04.780
Until next time, keep questioning the history of your code.

